    <h1>Chinese vs Indian Dried Chilli: Which Origin to Source</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      China and India dominate world chilli supply, but they are not interchangeable. This guide
      compares the two origins by variety, heat, colour, aroma and application, so you can choose on
      product fit rather than habit.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Choose Chinese chilli for colour-forward and aromatic applications
      with controlled heat (Jinta, Zidantou, Erjingtiao); choose Indian chilli when you need a specific
      heat profile — from colour-grade Kashmiri and Byadgi to the very-hot Teja and super-hot varieties.
      The origin sets the variety toolbox, not a single heat level.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The two origins at a glance</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dimension</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Chinese chilli</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Indian chilli</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Signature varieties</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Jinta, Zidantou (colour); Erjingtiao (aroma); Sanying, Jixin, Xiaomi (heat)</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Kashmiri, Byadgi (colour); Guntur Sannam, Teja (heat); Bhut Jolokia (super-hot)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Heat range</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Mainly low-to-high medium; controlled, consistent</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Full spectrum — mild to super-hot</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Colour (ASTA)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Strong — high-ASTA colour varieties</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Byadgi/Kashmiri are the colour workhorses</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Typical use</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Colour, aroma, controlled-heat blends, paprika-type</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Heat-dominant dishes, curry, oleoresin, chilli extract</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Where Chinese chilli wins</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Colour</strong> — high-ASTA varieties (Jinta, Zidantou) and paprika-grade material.</li>
      <li><strong>Aroma</strong> — Erjingtiao is a fragrance variety with no Indian equivalent in the same
      role.</li>
      <li><strong>Controlled, consistent heat</strong> — the Chinese culinary spectrum sits mostly in the
      medium-to-high band, well suited to seasoning blends and hot sauces.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Where Indian chilli wins</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Heat depth</strong> — from Kashmiri's mild colour to Teja and super-hots; unmatched
      heat ceiling.</li>
      <li><strong>Oleoresin and extract</strong> — India is the dominant source for capsaicin oleoresin
      and chilli extract.</li>
      <li><strong>Specific regional profiles</strong> — Guntur Sannam for balanced heat-colour, Byadgi for
      deep-red colour.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The decision rule</h2>
    <p>
      Choose by what the finished product is scored on. If colour and aroma carry the product — a
      seasoning blend, a colour-forward sauce — Chinese varieties give you high ASTA and controlled heat.
      If heat is the point — a curry, an extract, an extreme-heat product — Indian varieties give you the
      heat ceiling and the oleoresin supply chain. Many industrial buyers source <em>both</em>, blending
      Chinese colour with Indian heat.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Limitations and notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Both origins vary by region and crop year — always specify variety and origin, not just the
      country.</li>
      <li>Market supply and export rules change; confirm current conditions and the destination's
      food-safety limits with your supplier.</li>
      <li>This is a variety-level comparison — not a claim that one country is uniformly "hotter" or
      "better".</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The Author's Take</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Position:</strong> In my view, "Chinese vs Indian chilli" is the wrong question — the
      right question is which variety does your product need, and the answer is usually a blend of
      origins.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reasoning:</strong> No single origin optimises heat, colour and aroma at once. Chinese
      varieties give you controlled heat and strong colour; Indian varieties give you the heat ceiling
      and oleoresin. Buyers who choose a country instead of a variety end up with a compromise; buyers
      who specify the variety get the profile they actually want, from whichever origin grows it best.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as a dried-chilli supplier's technical team,
      not a market or regulatory claim. Verify current supply and limits before contracting.
    </p>

